{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -A 65 -B 8 'boundary is drawn|one ABI is the principle|performance|Owner ruling' docs/design/datom.world.md | head -125; sed -n '620,660p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; sed -n '400,443p' docs/design/yang.wasm.md\"","aggregated_output":"88-([`yin.vm.linker.md`](./yin.vm.linker.md)). Second, every new guest substrate\n89-specifies its boundary as such a shim to the composition, never as a second\n90-system ABI, and a process-local ABI's definitions have exactly one home in the\n91-specification tree. Naming doctrine: \"the system ABI\" is reserved for\n92-`dao.stream`; a guest-host interface such as `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` is always\n93-qualified (the guest-host service ABI, the wasm service ABI), never bare \"the\n94-ABI\" in a definitional position.\n95-\n96:**Owner ruling, 2026-10-19: the boundary is drawn, not found; one ABI is the\n97-principle.** Where the line between machine and world sits above the\n98-`dao.stream` floor is a design choice, and it has been redrawn before\n99-(contract v4 promoted `:define` from host service to opcode). The sole valid\n100:justification for a second, process-local ABI is **performance**: the measured\n101-engineering cost of per-instruction boundary crossings. It is therefore an\n102-engineering-cost measure, not doctrine — provisional by principle, to be\n103-shrunk as measurements allow and dissolved where a lane can sit at the floor\n104-(a guest whose engine is compiled inside it needs only `dao.stream`). The\n105-floor itself is not movable: operations whose answers are facts about the\n106-world cannot be computed by shipped code. Architecture review should treat\n107-every addition to a process-local ABI as a cost to be justified, and every\n108-removal as progress toward the one-ABI state.\n109-\n110-**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\n111-happen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\n112-appends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\n113-identity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\n114-no implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\n115-correct outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n116-\n117-**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\n118-a timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\n119-nothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\n120-Constructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\n121-to data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n122-\n123-**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\n124-adapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\n125-timers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\n126-what they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\n127-the append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\n128-to the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\n129-deposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\n130-may close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\n131-not an upward callback.\n132-\n133-Host types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\n134-place holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\n135-transport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\n136-additive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\n137-event mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\n138-for facts it owns.\n139-\n140-Which stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\n141-adapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\n142-transport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\n143-the layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\n144-constructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n145-\n146-A transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\n147-none of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\n148-happen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\n149-event. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n150-(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\n151-does not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\n152-not yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\n153-interpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n154-\n155-Two things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\n156-call isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\n157-appears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\n158-callback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\n159-that callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\n160-directions wearing the shape of an event.\n161-\n162-## Architectural Commitments\n163-\n164-- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n165-- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n\n### E.3 Service protocol: `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\n\n*(Owner amendment note, 2026-10-19: By explicit owner ruling, the service\nABI is named `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`, renamed from `yin.register.host.v1` in\nr5 §4.1.1, and its canonical tables live in this section. Other\nspecifications reference these tables rather than duplicating them.)*\n\nThe following names define the **engine host implementation ABI**\n(`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`, r5 §4.1.1), not repository functions or a guest\ninstruction-set schema change. This protocol is a process-local shim, not a\nsecond system ABI: it terminates at the embedding composition, which alone\ntranslates service requests into `dao.stream` operations — `dao.stream` remains\nthe only contract that crosses a trust domain (`datom.world.md`, Streams → Host\nBoundaries → The system ABI). Owner ruling, 2026-10-19: this ABI's sole\njustification is **performance** — the measured cost of per-instruction\nboundary crossings. It is a cost measure, not doctrine: shrink it toward the\n`dao.stream` floor as measurements allow (fable's W-gate plan: crossings only\nfor effects, GC, and capability minting), and treat the engine-in-guest lane —\nwhose only interface is `dao.stream` — as the strategic endpoint at which this\nABI dissolves. All runtimes supply identical imports. The\nshared runtime module imports `memory` (one wasm32, non-shared memory),\n`functions` (one original function table), and\n**`exchange(control_ptr: i32) -> i32`** from module name\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`. Generated component modules import the same\nmemory/table and the service entry as needed, plus declared immutable `i32`\nbase-offset words. No import/export passes a JS value, `externref`, Rust\npointer, string object, multi-value result or `i64` through a host-call\nsignature. `i64` pcs/numbers are bytes in memory; core `i64` computation\ninside wasm is allowed.\n\n#### E.3.1 Offsets and ownership\n\nReserve bytes `0–255` for ABI control, initially zeroed; `control_ptr` is `0`\nfor v1. At offsets `+0`/`+4`/`+8`/`+12` store little-endian `u32` magic\n`0x31524e59`, major `1`, minor `0`, header length `64`. Offsets `+16`/`+20`\nhold request pointer/length; `+24`/`+28` response pointer/capacity; `+32`\nResponse written length; `+36` required response length; `+40`/`+44` exchange\nsequence low/high; `+48` transport status; `+52` flags (zero in v1);\n`+56`/`+60` reserved zero. Initial request region is `[256, 32768)`, response\nregion `[32768, 65536)`; task allocation starts at `65536`. For larger\nHost-side payload materialization, the 5-step `register-restore` algorithm, and cross-layout\ntransport refusals are specified in [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.2.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Target interface specification: Import ABI `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\n\n*(Owner amendment note, 2026-10-19: By explicit owner ruling, the service import ABI is named\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` — renamed from `yin.register.host.v1` in r5 §4.1.1 — and its canonical\nprotocol tables live in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3. This section specifies the\ncompiler's target-interface binding and references those tables rather than duplicating them.)*\n\nEmitted WebAssembly modules target the guest-agnostic engine service protocol\n**`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`** defined in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3 (r5 §4.1.1).\nThe emitted module never observes `dao.stream` directly — a wasm module cannot\nexpress a stream operation — so this protocol is the process-local shim between\nthe guest and the embedding composition, which alone speaks the system ABI\n(`datom.world.md`, Streams → Host Boundaries → The system ABI). Owner ruling,\n2026-10-19: this guest-host ABI exists **only as an engineering-cost measure**\n(per-instruction crossing cost), never as doctrine; the engine-in-guest lane,\nwhose sole interface is `dao.stream`, is the strategic endpoint at which it\ndissolves, and W-gates shrink it in the meantime:\n\n1. **Module import bindings ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3):** The shared runtime module imports `memory` (one wasm32, non-shared memory), `functions` (one original function table), and **`exchange(control_ptr: i32) -> i32`** from module name `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`. Generated component modules import the same `memory`/`functions` and the service entry as needed, plus declared immutable `i32` base-offset words. No import or export passes a JS value, `externref`, Rust pointer, string object, multi-value result, or `i64` through a host-call signature.\n2. **Control-block layout and buffer ownership ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.1):** Emitted service thunks address ABI control exactly as §E.3.1 defines — the control-block field/offset map, magic, version fields, header length, request/response buffer boundaries and task-allocation start are owned by that section, and the compiler must emit offsets consistent with it. Guest allocation/growth occurs between `exchange` calls, never inside `exchange`.\n3. **Transport statuses, execution statuses, and `fetch-response` ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.2):** Emitted service thunks handle the transport-status set and the response-retry rule exactly as §E.3.2 defines: on `NEED_SPACE` the guest thunk resizes the response buffer to the required byte length and issues a `fetch-response` for the same sequence number without re-executing or re-sending the semantic transition. Decoded responses carry the private scheduling status set of §E.3.2.\n4. **TLV framing, expanded-canonical-bytes ordering, and Float64/NaN encoding ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.3):** All request/response payloads and constant/value definitions emitted or decoded by the runtime module obey §E.3.3's single outer framing rule, its expanded-canonical-bytes ordering rule (map keys and set elements sorted before tag-`13` handle compression), and its Float64/NaN canonical encoding.\n5. **Message envelope, register snapshot fields, and compiled vs. service division ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.4, [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.1):** Each service request carries the materialized register execution snapshot (`pc`/`registers`/`frames`/`K`/`store-of`/`value`/`halted`/`blocked` plus layout generation) before any service that can collect or park. External continuation export resolves tag-`13` arena references to the payload values in §3.5 or refuses. Pure body control, register moves, and guest closure calls stay compiled in the emitted wasm module; the host service is never allowed to “run the remaining program” to manufacture the expected trace.\n\nThe host-side implementation of `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`, the **single-engine-port rule**, and the\n“one transition implementation, two dispatchers” rule are specified in\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.1 and §E.4.2.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Per-image-row compilation, relocation, and artifact-cache law\n\nCompile each independently admitted image into a wasm Module whose body entry functions import\nthe same `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI memory and services. Instantiate it for the receiving task\nand install its functions into the task's imported function table using the runtime embedding\nAPI. This table maps implementation body ids to uniform functions; it is not the guest offset\ntable.\n\nAttach is a staged, append-only transaction:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"**The ruling changes my recommendation, but it does not make “performance is the sole valid justification” a sound general architectural law.** I now favor engine-in-guest as the planned destination, with the split-machine service interface as an explicitly temporary alternative. I would move the node-lane feasibility work and comparison into W0, rather than first investigate it in W6.\n\nMy round-1 argument established why a private interface is needed **after choosing to split the machine**. It did not establish why that split should be chosen. The owner is right to challenge that circularity.\n\n**1. What I retain, and what I revise**\n\nI retain three conclusions:\n\n- Machine-internal operations should not automatically become stream history.\n- Rich internal values still require an internal representation; canonical wire encoding cannot replace it wholesale.\n- Imported functions can implement `dao.stream` operations. Wasm’s use of imports does not require a different semantic protocol.\n\nI revise the status of the “indispensable reason.” Shared state and richer values justify the interface *conditional on host ownership of that state*. Move ownership into the guest, and the justification disappears. The representation problem remains, but it becomes an internal implementation problem rather than a guest-host protocol problem.\n\nI also qualify the argument that “FFI yields but primitive application does not.” That is a property of the current instruction contract, not a fundamental distinction between arithmetic and the world. Internal suspension could be hidden inside a logical instruction if the implementation preserves its observable atomicity, ordering and refusal behavior. That would require careful scheduling and buffering; it is not impossible.\n\nHowever, appending every primitive request to an observable stream would introduce new history. “Any operation can use request/response” does not mean “doing so preserves the existing observable contract.” The stronger route to one ABI is **moving internal execution inside the guest**, not converting internal execution into external effects.\n\n**2. Where I disagree with the ruling**\n\nThe [recorded ruling](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/datom.world.md:96) combines two different claims:\n\n1. Prefer one semantic interoperability contract.\n2. Runtime performance is the only legitimate reason for any other interface.\n\nI support the first. The second is too narrow.\n\n| Candidate reason | My assessment |\n|---|---|\n| Capability/security granularity | A genuine reason for an isolation boundary, but usually **not** for a second semantic ABI. Stream handles, capability restrictions and authorized interpreters can provide the granularity. A generic service interface may actually expose more authority. |\n| Determinism and cross-embedder parity | Acceptance requirements, not reasons to split the engine. Shipping the same engine inside wasm may reduce host-dependent variation. |\n| ISA freeze versus extensibility | Usually not a second-ABI justification. Versioned primitives and effect vocabularies can evolve without making every operation an opcode. A service interface moves the compatibility obligation; it does not remove it. |\n| Failure isolation | Can independently justify a component or process boundary. It does not inherently require a second operation vocabulary: streams can connect isolated components. Fault containment itself is not reducible to throughput. |\n| Independent versioning and existing-host compatibility | Can genuinely justify a private binding when independently supplied components must interoperate through an already fixed interface. Replacing that integration with stream messages may be infeasible or alter its contract. This is a compatibility/lifecycle reason, not necessarily a crossing-performance reason. |\n| Implementation and deployment cost | A legitimate reason for an interim split: runtime availability, debugging, binary size, startup, integration effort and delivery risk matter. These are engineering costs, but they are not all “measured per-instruction boundary crossings.” |\n\nFor this project, I do **not** see a demonstrated compatibility requirement that makes the broad engine service ABI permanently necessary. The shared Rust crates and explicit state model make engine-in-guest a credible destination. But that is a project-specific conclusion, not proof that all private ABIs are justified only by runtime speed.\n\n“Every removal is progress” also needs qualification. Removing a documented interface by introducing implicit shared-memory layout dependence is regression. Removing it by colocating the owning implementation is progress. Count independently maintained contracts and coupling, not just imported function names.\n\nThere is another important measurement issue: the broad service ABI is potentially faster than **streaming every internal operation**, but potentially slower than **executing those operations inside wasm**. It cannot justify itself solely by winning against the first, unattractive alternative. The performance comparison must include the node lane.\n\n**3. The floor is an information constraint, not a fixed architectural location**\n\nThe sound part is:\n\n> A computation cannot produce information that is absent from its initial state and subsequent inputs.\n\nThat explains why genuinely external observations require ingress. It does not establish a permanently fixed list of “world-fact operations.”\n\nA recorded clock, deterministic random generator, supplied filesystem snapshot or replayed network transcript can turn previously external queries into local computation. The operation’s name may remain unchanged while its meaning changes from “observe the current world” to “consult this recorded world.”\n\nReplication does not abolish the information boundary either. Agreement requires messages from participants, unless their decisions are already part of the supplied state. Consensus can establish an agreed fact; it cannot locally reveal an unobserved external event.\n\nThus the floor moves when the system changes its inputs, authority or promised semantics. What remains immovable is the need for input when the required information is genuinely unavailable locally.\n\nAnd information theory does not uniquely imply `dao.stream`. **Choosing `dao.stream` as the sole representation of external interaction is the project’s architectural principle.** It is a strong, coherent choice, but it is not a consequence of wasm or the impossibility of computing unknown facts.\n\nAn engine-in-guest node will still need a physical binding: imports, handles, buffers, outcome representation and lifecycle control. “One ABI” should mean that this binding implements the stream contract without adding a second engine-service vocabulary—not that all representation conventions disappear.\n\n**4. I would change W0–W6**\n\nThe current body-lane-first schedule no longer fits the stated strategic endpoint without an early decision gate. Building the full TLV service system through W5 and only then testing whether it was needed risks making the provisional architecture permanent through investment.\n\nAlso, **compiled bodies versus interpreter dispatch and engine-in-guest versus engine-in-host are separate choices**. The node lane can eventually execute compiled body functions. It need not permanently replace lowering with an interpreter.\n\nI would revise the schedule as follows:\n\n| Slice | Change I recommend |\n|---|---|\n| **W0** | Preserve the shared K0 kit, exact codecs, native-runtime probes and feature profile. Add a minimal engine-in-guest feasibility prototype using the shared Rust implementation, alongside a minimal split-engine prototype. Exercise pure execution, snapshot/restore and one blocking stream cycle. Measure crossings, encoding, execution time, startup, memory and artifact size. Do not first build the complete service vocabulary. |\n| **W1** | Establish pure register execution with guest-owned values and state in the node prototype. Use interpreter dispatch initially if that accelerates validation. Prove primitive semantics, allocation, refusal atomicity and bounded tail execution. Compile the same binary for Node and native-runtime testing. |\n| **W2** | Implement continuations and restore against that ownership model. Preserve all existing payload, check-order and cross-lane tests. Private handles remain internal; export materializes portable data or refuses. |\n| **W3** | Integrate stream operations, scheduler behavior, custody and FFI. Guest execution returns blocked state to the driver; imported operations do not retain suspended guest stacks. Keep every current once-only and terminal-outcome test. |\n| **W4** | Add module growth, closure transport and artifact integration; retain Wasmer and bidirectional module-slice gates. Demonstrate compiled bodies calling the colocated engine implementation without a host service-message round trip for internal transitions. |\n| **W5** | Qualify the node lane against the complete kit. A split-engine lane may ship too, but its retained private services require explicit evidence and scope—not inheritance from the original design. |\n| **W6** | Optimize dispatch, spilling, batching and runtime placement using measured workloads. This is where optimization belongs; it should not be the first investigation of the strategic architecture. |\n\nThis is a proposal to amend the plan, not a claim that those shared crates are already wasm-ready. W0 may uncover substantial obstacles. Under the ruling, those obstacles should be reported explicitly; they should not be disguised as measured performance findings.\n\nFinally, the statement still present in [yang.wasm §4](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.wasm.md)—that a wasm module cannot express a stream operation—should be revised. It contradicts the proposed destination. Wasm needs a binding to express stream operations; it does not need an engine service protocol to do so.\n\n**My revised position is: plan for guest-owned execution and a `dao.stream`-only semantic boundary, and require the split-engine alternative to earn its place in W0.** The owner’s direction is persuasive. The absolute claim that performance is the only legitimate consideration is not."}}
